        <!-- Single toggle for the React build (frontend-maven-plugin), mirroring Turing.
             Default false = build the console; pass -Dskip.npm=true to skip it and run
             against a previously-built src/main/resources/public. -->
        <skip.npm>false</skip.npm>
        <!-- SH250 — surefire's `@{argLine}` is a *late* replacement of this property, so it has
             to exist even when nothing has set it, or the build fails resolving it. Declared empty
             here so the Mockito agent below can append rather than replace: the next tool that
             wants the JVM's command line (JaCoCo is the likely one) sets `argLine` and both agents
             end up on it, which is the part the task note said would actually take care. -->
        <argLine></argLine>
        <!-- SH415 — the ITs are opt-in: `verify` skips them, `-DskipITs=false` runs them.
             Failsafe reads this property itself (`@Parameter(property = "skipITs")`), so the
             default lives here and needs no plugin config to enforce.

             Measured before flipping it: the failsafe phase is 31.1 s of a 120 s `verify`, and
             16.2 s of that is ShAgentTenantIsolationIT — *one test* — because the first context
             boot in a JVM costs 13–35 s where every later one costs ~1.5 s (SH414). So the phase
             is mostly a cold start, and it is paid by whoever types `verify` for a reason
             unrelated to tenancy.

             The reason this is safe is not the 31 s, and it is worth writing down because it is
             the opposite of what it looks like. **These ITs were never a gate.** No workflow runs
             the Java suite at all: `build.yml` is the only one that calls Maven `verify`, it is
             `workflow_dispatch` (manual), and it passes `-Dmaven.test.skip=true`. Failsafe also
             carries `testFailureIgnore=true`, so a red IT does not fail the build even when one
             runs. Nothing is being switched off here that was catching anything; what changes is
             that the run now *says* the ITs did not run, instead of the developer assuming from
             `verify` that they did.

             Two things that follow, and neither is a reason to leave the default at false.
             Six of the 26 IT tests (ShPostgresSchemaIT, ShMariaDbSchemaIT) were not running in
             either direction: without a Docker daemon they skip, by their own
             @Testcontainers(disabledWithoutDocker = true), and *with* one they error — SH416,
             measured, cause not yet found. So "verify runs the cross-DB checks" was already false
             on any machine without a daemon and is false with one. And the honest fix is to wire
             and PR, which this makes *easier* to reason about: the unit suite is the fast gate
             (68 s, four forks, SH414) and the ITs are the deliberate second one, rather than one
             41 s blob that no workflow can afford to run on every push. -->
        <skipITs>true</skipITs>
