        <!--
          SH183 — the render path, and the removal of the thing it replaces.

          `org.openjdk.nashorn:nashorn-core` 15.7 used to be declared here with nothing
          calling it: the FSP engine that evaluated layouts through it was deleted years
          ago and only a `ShWebsiteProperties.nashorn` string array still named it. A
          script engine on the classpath that no code reaches is build weight and attack
          surface for free, so it goes.

          Handlebars.java is what takes its place, and it is the *same language authors
          already wrote* — the old engine loaded `handlebars.min.js` from a CDN at render
          time and compiled the layout with it, so a pure-Java implementation runs the
          existing templates with no script engine at all.

          The exclusion is the load-bearing part. Handlebars.java declares `nashorn-core`
          at compile scope for its optional JavaScript-helper integration
          (`registerHelpers(File)` and friends) — precisely the capability the Block D
          non-goal forbids, which would have arrived transitively while the direct
          declaration above was being deleted. Excluding it means the JS helper path is
          absent from the classpath rather than merely unused: a template cannot reach a
          script engine because there is no script engine to reach, which is the "by
          construction rather than by a filter someone has to remember to configure"
          the design asks for. ShRenderNoScriptEngineTest fails the build if it comes back.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.github.jknack</groupId>
            <artifactId>handlebars</artifactId>
            <version>4.5.5</version>
            <exclusions>
                <exclusion>
                    <groupId>org.openjdk.nashorn</groupId>
                    <artifactId>nashorn-core</artifactId>
                </exclusion>
            </exclusions>
        </dependency>
        <!--
          SH183 — declared explicitly rather than inherited. jsoup is already on the
          classpath via `tika-parser-html-module`, and the region composer parses a
          layout's HTML with it; a render path that depends on a *transitive* parser is
          one Tika bump away from not compiling, for a reason nothing would connect to
          rendering. Version left to Spring Boot's dependency management.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.jsoup</groupId>
            <artifactId>jsoup</artifactId>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>
